Situation:
The brigade is conducting sustained operations across a contested corridor. Maneuver elements are dispersed at forward operating bases and patrol bases, drawing critical commodities from a network of forward depots. Ground lines of communication are under intermittent interdiction from indirect fire and improvised explosive devices. Weather limits air resupply; ground convoys are the main effort. Some routes traverse defiles and choke points subject to observation, requiring route clearance, electronic warfare support, and convoy security when used. Depots have finite issue rates by commodity each period, and several units are reporting rising usage driven by ongoing operations.

Mission:
Deliver required quantities of priority supplies from Supply Depot Alpha, Supply Depot Bravo, and Distribution Node Charlie to engaged units (e.g., Maneuver Task Force Vanguard, Armored Cavalry Squadron Saber, Light Infantry Battalion Falcon, Artillery Battery Thunder, Reconnaissance Company Ghost, Air Defense Platoon Guardian, Engineer Company Pioneer) over the planning horizon at minimum total operational cost while preserving freedom of maneuver and force protection.

Execution/Concept:
- We will execute a multi-depot, multi-commodity ground distribution plan across the planning horizon. Depots will issue within their period and commodity limits; unit demands will be met to the maximum extent practical. Where tactical risk or capacity constraints preclude full delivery, controlled shortfalls may be accepted with an associated penalty cost.
- Convoy movement will be planned by route and period. Vehicle allocations will be integer, and total vehicles employed across all routes each period will not exceed the available fleet. The mass of commodities moved on any route is bounded by vehicle capacity and the number of vehicles assigned.
- Activating a route (dispatching any vehicles on it in a period) triggers a fixed Risk Cost for that route in that period, representing security overhead (escort teams, route clearance packages, ISR tasking). This fixed risk cost is incurred regardless of the number of vehicles sent on that route. Variable transport costs apply per movement along active routes.
- Cost components to minimize are: variable transport costs, fixed route activation risk costs, per-vehicle deployment costs (fuel, maintenance, crews), and shortage penalties for any unmet demand. The plan will concentrate loads to reduce the count of active routes where feasible, favoring more secure lines of communication, and will sequence deliveries to protect high-priority units while respecting depot constraints and fleet limits.

